>Hello, Kent.

>First observation: For all your buffing up, I notice you have no source
>of magic resistance whatsoever.

Bad. Very bad!

>You need to fix this ASAP.  You're intensely vulnerable to touch of
>death or armor destruction.

Bingo!

>2. You have a decent melee weapon,  and some good range weapons. This
>is good.

A healer is only going to be a so so fighter anyway. Buff up on staff.

>3. Am I correct in guessing that almost all your AC reduction is from
>your armor?
>If you have not done so already, find a peaceful priest and start
>dumping gold on him in shots of 400 * XL < your donation < 600 * XL

That is the first order of business for each and every one of my characters.
I generally play pacifist until I get my protection. Let my pet buff up to
level 9, kill as little as possible. Get the extra hit points by praying,
putting on as much armor as I can find, clean out all the shops from levels
1-4 and in Minetown. Pay the minetown priest to get a naked AC of 1. Then
I start to buff up. 

Magic resistance is next on the agenda after that.

>4.  You have little in the way of light sources. I would recommend
>learning it as a spell.  Light keeps me alive where nothing else
>does... keeps me from blundering into critters in the dark.

In my current game I actually have a magic lamp that I haven't rubbed.
You'll find one in minetown sometimes. However in a real early game, the
wish is more important than the light.

>5. What are your resistances and intrinsics? Do you have fire
>resistance, shock resistance, cold resistance, sleep resistance,
>disintegration resistance?  If you do not, get 'em.

He's crowned. So he has everything but disintegration. Reflection will
protect him from black dragons once he starts to encounter them. IIRC
the Healer quest is chock full of dragons.

>6. fight ochre jellies at a distance.  Also mumaks. Lost good,
>promising characters to them.

Until I get good spellcasting under control I untrap all dart traps and
enchant to +3 or +4.

Sleep is also excellent for a lot of these tougher monsters.

>7. How many pets do you have?   There's nothing like having a pack of
>dogs -- unless it's a pack of archons -- and a magic whistle. You can
>use tripe rations as a cheap 'charm monster'.

Pets are a personal preference. I absolutely depend on them in the early
game. In midgame after I get magic resistance I usually try to poly my pet(s)
into something useful. I love Umber Hulks.

But eventually they really do nothing but get in the way. At that point
you abandon them. In my current game I think I left Garfield the crocodile
on LVL 11 or somesuch.

>8. your hit points could be higher. Consider quaffing ! of extra
>healing. I would avoid nurse dancing.

Use alchemy to turn those extra healings into full healings. In my current
game I have like 23 potions of full healing. I also can cast cure sickness 
and extra healing at 0%

My current character has 468 hit points. The vast majority of those are via
golden glows granted by my god.


>9. I see you have no wands.   Recommend saving wands of fire, wands of
>lightning, wands of digging, and wands of teleport.
>
>Wands of fire can be used to engrave "Elbereth" in 1 turn. Also, the
>engraving made with a wand of fire will last forever. You would be
>amazed how often this will save your life.

I used this for my pudding farming in my current game. I burned 3 wands
engraving all around the altar then read a scroll of earth to get
boulders all the way around.

>Wands of lightning engrave in 1 turn, last a real long time, but blind
>you.

That's what unihorns are for.

>Wands of digging write in 1 turn,  are common as dirt in Gehenna, and
>the engraving will last for a few turns.

I have about 20 of them. Plan to burn them out on the plane of Earth.

>Wands of teleport are almost as good as wands of death -- they work on
>anything, they hardly ever miss,  and whatever you're shooting at is
>going to stop being an immediate problem. In some ways it's better than
>a wand of death, because a teleport wand will work on vampires, liches,
>and demons which a wand of death will not.

Interesting. I usually use them on myself to extricate myself out of a
tough spot.

>Of course, you will have to fight the creature again, but it will stop
>bothering for a few turns ... possibly quite a few if it was sent to
>the other end of the dungeon.  You can then
>take it on after you've solved your immediate problems, and instead of
>being swarmed it is a one-on-one unfair fight.  Or just run away, going
>around it to the stairs.

All excellent points. It's amazing how many problems in Nethack can 
actually be solved by simply running away. I think that it hurts many
players egos to do so though.

>10. I see no means of levitation.  Get one -- not only is it useful on
>the planes of air and fire, but if you use it a lot you can float over
>trap doors, pits and such like.

I'll be necessary in the end game. But it isn't critical until the
late late game. Also Xanthian has a amulet of water breathing. So he
can use it for critical mid game levels such as Medusa and the Castle.

>11. Consider getting boots of water walking.  Then you can walk on a
>moat and dip as many potions as you like in the moat and it'll never
>dry out. I get 40-50 potions of water that way (which, of course,
>become Holy water in short order).

Too slow. Jumping in with the potions will give you the same number
of waters in just a few turns. Cancellation is also excellent for
wiping out scrolls, spellbooks, and potions.

