Stupidity! Like eating a gelatenous cube will Satiated! DOH!

>14. A suggestion that helps me the most ... it's a tactic ...

>BE A COWARD.

Especially in the early game. I play pacifist until I get my naked AC
down to 1. Those tactics are helpful later in the game because you realize
that you can in fact avoid most issues.

>Let your pets do the fighting for you.  Use polearms.  Use ranged
>weapons.  Run away. Engrave "Elbereth". Drop scrolls of scare monster.
>Read scrolls of teleportation. Zap the floor underneath you with a wand
>of digging and fall to the next level.  Zap the wall and go through the
>wall. Zap a wand of teleport at bad guys to make them go away.  Zap it
>at yourself to escape.  Do anything and everything you reasonably can
>to avoid melee combat.   Consider playing a rogue or a ranger to get
>used to that playing style.

All excellent suggestions. At least until you can get equipped enough where
you can afford to stand toe to toe.

>15. Remember ... this is a TURN-based game.

>Your single greatest advantage is not your +7 artifact weapon, is not
>your Wand of Mass Destruction, is not your +35 Chromatic Dragon Scale
>Mail.  Your greatest advantage in a dungeon where you are outnumbered
>5,000 to 1 is the gray matter on top of your shoulders ... and ALL THE
>TIME IN THE WORLD to use it.

I've learned not to play when I get tired. I just save and come back to
it at another time. It does give you a chance to think about what you
need to do instead of blundering into YASD.

>So ... if you get in a jam, STOP!  Walk away from the computer. Smoke a
>cigarette or eat a twinkie.  Watch a movie.  Let your blood settle
>down.  THEN come back, look at your inventory,  sit down with a paper
>and pencil, and THINK your way out of your predicament.

Excellent suggestions. All except for the ciggie!

>NOTHING HAPPENS UNTIL YOU PRESS A KEY.   You have as much time as you
>like.  Use all of it.   Your greatest advantage is something you'll
>never have in real combat ... the luxury to think through your
>situation.

>And if you're dying often from blundering into things in the corridors,
>the time has come to start thinking between moves as well ...  press a
>"move key", count to five mississippi, then press a movement key again.

That's the toughest part of the game to learn. You get used to keying fast.
It takes time to learn to recognize that you have to slow it down.

>Don't simply run around like this was DOOM or an archlich will
>touch-of-death you to eternity before you even know he's there.

>This is going to sound silly ... but 19 years?  Adding you to my prayer
>list.  Usually reserve it for emergencies like surgeries and so forth,
>but I think this qualifies.

Agreed. But I think we've all outlined some essential changes in strategy
that will help:

1) Magic Resistance is crucial.
2) low AC is essential.
3) Unless your character is a combat wombat type work towards becoming the
most effective spellcaster you can.
4) Melee isn't necessarily going to win you the game.
5) Think. Think. and think again.

